# Response headers for the static site.
#
# Parsed by Workers static assets — like `_redirects`, this file is not itself
# served. Redirects run first, so a path matching both is redirected and never
# reaches these rules.
#
# **The point of this file is that the cookies page stops being a promise.**
# That page says nothing is fetched from anywhere else and there is nothing to
# consent to, which is true today and true only for as long as nobody pastes a
# tag manager in. The policy below makes it enforced by the browser instead: a
# third-party script added later does not quietly start working, it fails.
#
# **A per-path `connect-src` was tried and measured wrong.** A second `/*`-
# style block scoped to `/en-us/careers/apply/*` looked like a narrower
# exception, but Workers static assets does not replace a header a more
# general matching block already set — it sends both. Two
# `Content-Security-Policy` headers are two policies in force at once, and the
# spec intersects them: `connect-src 'none'` from the first still won, so
# careers.js's fetches failed with no console line to explain why (a blocked
# `connect-src` is reported through a different channel than `console.*`).
# Checked with `curl -D -` against the live path before concluding this,
# twice. One policy, site-wide, is the only version that is actually enforced
# as written.

/*
  # `default-src 'self'` covers everything not named below.
  #
  # `style-src` needs 'unsafe-inline' because a handful of callouts carry a
  # `style="margin:0"` attribute. Those could be moved into styles.css and the
  # keyword dropped; until they are, this is honest about why it is here.
  #
  # `connect-src` names exactly two things: `'self'`, and the project's own
  # Supabase URL for `/en-us/careers/apply/`'s two fetches (an insert, a
  # resume upload) — never a wildcard, never any other origin. The anon key
  # careers.js carries is `sb_publishable_…`, the same public-by-intent value
  # AppConfig.swift ships; it is not a secret and grants only what the
  # `anon`-scoped RLS policies on `job_applications` and the resume bucket
  # allow, which is insert and nothing else. Every other page still makes no
  # request at all — this only widens who is *allowed* to be asked, not who
  # is.
  #
  # `frame-ancestors 'none'` stops the site being framed inside somebody else's
  # page, which for a document a reviewer is asked to trust is worth refusing.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://fwnezkbesjoazlpaflbq.supabase.co; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests

  # No sniffing a served type into something else.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

  # Outbound links go to Google, YouTube and Supabase. They get the origin, not
  # the path — nobody needs to know which of our pages somebody left from.
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

  # Nothing here uses any of these, so nothing here should be able to ask.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()

  # Two years, subdomains included. Deliberately without `preload`: that is a
  # submission to a browser-vendor list and is slow to undo, and it should be a
  # decision taken on its own rather than a side effect of adding a header file.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains
